                <pb facs="0137" n="131" sameAs="frus:frus1939v04/pg131"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102 Kulangsu/188: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in Japan</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Dooman</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-07-06T20:00:00-04:00">July 6, 1939—8 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>184. Your 293, June 23, 3 p.m., and Amoy’s 64, July 2, noon, and 66, July 5, 3
                    p.m. As 2 weeks have elapsed since you left your informal memorandum at the
                    Foreign Office, it would seem that there has been sufficient time for Sawada to
                    familiarize himself with the situation and give you a reply. The Department
                    accordingly suggests that, in your discretion, you make another approach to the
                    Foreign Office in this matter.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Chungking and Amoy.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
